     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi,<div><br></div><div>I really like featureCounts so far, it&#39;s very fast (in most situtations) and easy to use. Thanks! </div><div><br></div><div>However, I&#39;m experiencing some issues with a very large annotation file. I&#39;m mapping reads to a large gene catalog (more than 11 million genes) and I have a simple GTF annotation file for the catalog that annotates each reference sequence across its whole length with a &quot;gene_id&quot; attribute. The complete GTF file is 996 MB in size, and contains annotations for 11,659,115 individual gene sequences. I realize this might not be the intended use-case for featureCounts, but I really like it and would love to use it for this as well. </div><div><br></div><div>Running featureCounts using only a small subset of the annotation file works well. I first tried with a subset containing only the first 50k annotations, then 100k, and at 250k it started to run fairly slowly, but when I tried with an annotation file containing 500k of the lines from the full file featureCounts really chokes. It sits forever (I got tired of waiting after an hour or so). I have an idea of what could be causing the issue; maybe it&#39;s due to the disparity between the number of reference sequences (what you call Features) and different &quot;gene_id&quot; variants (what you call Meta-features). There are only 7252 different meta-features in the full annotation file. </div><div><br></div><div>Here&#39;s the output from featureCounts when I try to run using the whole annotation file:</div><div><br></div><div><div>      </div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">                                                                          <br>        </span><span style=\"color:#660\">==========</span><span style=\"color:#000\">     _____ _    _ ____  _____  ______          _____         